course: dictionary
lesson: 10677
title: Official and unofficial words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Official means approved or announced in a formal way by the people in charge. Add un- and you get the opposite, so unofficially means not in an official or formal way. «Only about a dozen countries officially recognise Taiwan as a country, though many more trade and talk with it unofficially.» The sentence sets officially against unofficially, so you can see the opposite meaning.
explain: Look for the clue word. The word though tells you there is a contrast: only a dozen countries say yes formally, but many more still trade and talk with Taiwan. So unofficially means doing something without formal approval. Test it by swapping in "without formal approval" and check that the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Unofficially can also mean privately or secretly, before something is officially said. When a news report says something is true unofficially, it is accepted or known, but nobody has formally confirmed it yet.
fact: Official means approved or announced in a formal way by people in charge.
fact: «Unofficially» means not in an official or formal way.
fact: «Unofficially» can also mean privately or secretly, before something is officially said.
fact: The prefix un- often turns a word into its opposite, so «officially» and «unofficially» are opposites.
fact: Look for clue words like «though», which show a contrast between two ideas.
exercise: «Only about a dozen countries officially recognise Taiwan as a country, though many more trade and talk with it unofficially.» What does unofficially mean here? | Only for one day | *Without formal approval, in an informal way | In a formal, approved way with official papers | Very loudly, in front of a crowd
exercise: Find and copy one word from the sentence «Only about a dozen countries officially recognise Taiwan as a country, though many more trade and talk with it unofficially.» that means the opposite of unofficially. | *officially | countries | dozen | Taiwan
exercise: A newspaper says, «Unofficially, the team has chosen its new captain.» What does this tell the reader? | The captain has been announced on the radio with a formal ceremony | The captain is a secret robot | *The captain is accepted, but has not been formally announced yet | Nobody has chosen a captain at all
test: Which meaning fits best? «The school unofficially cancelled the trip, but no letter has been sent home yet.» | Loudly, in front of everyone in the hall | In a formal letter with a stamp and a signature | Happily, with a big smile | *Before anything was formally announced
test: Which clue word in «Only about a dozen countries officially recognise Taiwan as a country, though many more trade and talk with it unofficially.» shows a contrast? | *though | dozen | Taiwan | countries
exam: Which sentence uses unofficially correctly? | The judge read the verdict unofficially to a full court, with a signed document on the desk. | The rain was unofficially heavy, so the match was officially cancelled. | *The coach unofficially chose Sam for the match, but the team list will not be posted until Friday.
